---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import CaseStudyBlock from '../../components/CaseStudyBlock.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="UX Research to evaluate instructional materials | sivani.me"
	description="Uncovering issues and opportunities in the instructional design of our B2B and B2C technical programs"
>
	<SiteHeader />
	<div class="content">
		<main>
			<section class="hero">
				<a class="back-link" href="/">
					<svg viewBox="0 0 17 17" width="17" height="17" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
						<path
							d="M9.1 3.7 L4.3 8.5 L9.1 13.3"
							stroke="currentColor"
							stroke-width="1"
							stroke-linecap="round"
							stroke-linejoin="round"
							fill="none"
						/>
						<path d="M4.3 8.5 H13.7" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" fill="none" />
					</svg>
					<span>Home</span>
				</a>
				<div class="hero__body">
					<div class="hero__heading">
						<h1>UX Research to evaluate instructional materials</h1>
						<p class="hero__company">Company: Code Chrysalis</p>
					</div>
					<div class="hero__image">
						<img src="/images/case-study-1/cover.png" alt="" />
					</div>
					<hr />
					<p>Uncovering issues and opportunities in the instructional design of our B2B and B2C technical programs</p>
					<hr />
					<p class="hero__skills">
						Skills: user interviews, usability tests, survey design, heuristic evaluations, data analysis, workshop
						facilitation, project management
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<SectionHeading text="Summary" />

			<section class="summary">
				<h2>Challenge</h2>
				<p>
					Identified a research gap: the Product team was investing a lot of time in improving the instructional
					materials used in our software engineering bootcamps, without any concrete knowledge to back up their
					assumptions on what made a good lecture slide deck or sprint tutorial.
				</p>

				<h2>Key insight</h2>
				<p>
					Different users had conflicting feedback about the information density desired in a lecture deck, but the
					core need underneath what they were expressing was the same: clarity.
				</p>

				<h2>Outcomes</h2>
				<ul>
					<li>Product team members reallocated their time more effectively.</li>
					<li>
						The design and structure of instructional materials was updated to suit differing needs across instructors
						and students.
					</li>
					<li>This impacted the approach to curriculum creation in both our B2C and B2B enterprise offerings.</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<SectionHeading text="Deep dive" />

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-1/context.png" title="Context">
				<p>
					Moving into my role as a Product Designer working on the Product team at Code Chrysalis gave me a
					well-substantiated respect and understanding for just how much time it took to craft the instructional
					materials for our programs. At every backlog meeting, I saw the breakdown of time it took per every slide
					deck, sprint readme, and syllabus. Our team was responsible for creating the instructional material for all
					our engineering, design, and product management bootcamps, for B2C students and B2B clients, including a
					global enterprise automotive client.
				</p>
				<p>
					Slide creation was incredibly time-consuming. I also saw the demand from the instructors for new decks when
					new tools released, or content became outdated - both of which tend to happen often in the software
					engineering and design spaces. Our instructional materials needed to be good, and they needed to be
					reevaluated in their efficacy on a regular basis.
				</p>
				<p>
					I was the first full-time designer hired at the company, and the only designer who'd ever worked on the
					Product team. I saw a golden research opportunity underlying an assumption everyone was making: that the
					slides even mattered at all. The thing most team members were spending all their time on: was it even
					working? Was it doing its job? What was the purpose of a singular slide deck, anyway?
				</p>
				<p>
					That's how the impulse for this research project was born. When I brought the idea up to my manager, the
					head of the Product team, she recognized the value this could kind of data could bring - a strong reasoning
					behind where their time was being spent.
				</p>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-1/my-role.png" title="My role">
				<p>
					As the only designer in our company, I carried out the full research study process on my own. This process
					included designing and conducting user interviews, usability studies, and custom surveys. After I processed
					the research, I shared my findings with my team and ran a solutions workshop with them to identify learnings
					and action items based on my findings.
				</p>
				<p>
					I was also encouraged by my manager to present this project to my company during one of our lunchtime
					company-wide knowledge sharing sessions, to help raise our collective design understanding and promote
					data-driven decision-making.
				</p>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-1/process.png" title="Process">
				<p>
					I combined a variety of research methods to collect a mix of quantitative and qualitative data, and
					segmented my participants into the three types of users who used our slides and other instructional
					materials: students (6 participants), instructors (4 participants), product team members (2 participants).
					Across all three groups, I included both first-language English speakers and non-FLE speakers.
				</p>
				<p>
					As the sole person carrying out this project, an inherent limitation was the potential for bias to creep in
					from the human interpreting all this data (me). I also was not able to include participants to represent
					every single program, since it was not possible to gain research access to some of our large corporate B2B
					clients.
				</p>
				<p><strong>How I mitigated the above:</strong></p>
				<ul>
					<li>Designed questions that were non-leading</li>
					<li>Had a team member provide review for the questions used (across the interviews, usability tests, and surveys)</li>
					<li>
						Used interview transcripts to reduce recall bias (that could come into play if I had solely relied on my
						notetaking or memory, especially given I was the sole facilitator present for these interviews)
					</li>
					<li>
						Conducted 12 studies, which exceeded the standard research validity benchmark – Jakob Nielsen's
						widely-cited finding (Nielsen Norman Group, 2000) that testing with 5 users uncovers approximately 85% of
						usability problems in an interface
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p><strong>Research methods:</strong></p>
				<ul>
					<li>User/stakeholder interviews</li>
					<li>Usability tests</li>
					<li>Custom survey</li>
					<li>Standardized "User Experience Questionnaire"</li>
					<li>Heuristic evaluation (17 slide decks / 41 issues found)</li>
					<li>Competitive analysis (14 materials)</li>
				</ul>
				<p><strong>Analysis methods:</strong></p>
				<ul>
					<li>Coding of interview transcripts (to reduce recall bias from relying on memory or notes alone)</li>
					<li>Thematic analysis (to arrange research findings into categories)</li>
				</ul>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-1/key-insights.png" title="Key insights">
				<p>
					One of the most compelling findings from the data I collected was a contradiction: some participants wanted
					more information on slides. Others wanted less. This was the case for both students and instructors.
				</p>
				<p>
					At first glance, this contradiction could seem unresolvable. Which group do we cater to? Favoring one,
					disadvantages another. But this is where it was important to look at this one finding in the context of all
					the other datapoints - interview responses also made clear the purpose behind how different participant
					groups used our slide decks. Many students used slides for study and review after class. Many instructors
					relied primarily on slide decks for lecture prep, in addition to using the slides for their primary use:
					presenting visual material during a lecture.
				</p>
				<p>
					While it is the responsibility of our curriculum materials as a whole to provide for all these use cases -
					should that responsibility fall solely on slides?
				</p>
				<p>
					So far, it had. Slides were: Lecture presentations (for students + instructors), Study guides (for
					students), Lesson plans (for instructors).
				</p>
				<p>
					Optimizing slides to be the perfect lesson plan for an instructor could sometimes make them serve as better
					study materials for the students that liked having a lot of detail up front. But this very same
					optimization could also make it harder for students that found the front-loading of such information in
					slides overwhelming.
				</p>
				<p>
					And I'm sure many of us have heard the rule of thumb that less is more when it comes to slides. No one wants
					to see a long series of paragraphs on a slide deck. Such dense information is better reserved for other
					formats.
				</p>
				<p>
					Slides can't do everything. Honing in on these research findings helped uncover the real problem underlying
					these opposing tensions: the information didn't have a volume issue so much as a clarity issue. Information
					needed to be clearer. Some people thought this meant more information was needed, some people thought this
					meant there should be minimal information. But the crux of the issue was clarity.
				</p>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-1/outcomes.png" title="Outcomes">
				<p>
					The moment these findings clicked, our team was able to pivot our strategy towards instructional design that
					would make better use of everyone's time: the team making the materials, and the people making use of them
					(students and instructors alike). By leading a solutions workshop for the Product team after sharing my
					findings, we were able to identify several concrete points on which to orient our future instructional
					design around: things we were already doing that we wanted to continue doing, and things we wanted to do
					differently.
				</p>
				<p><strong>The most impactful decisions we made:</strong></p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						Separate concerns across different curriculum materials – have guides as separate materials that function
						as detailed, nitty-gritty walkthroughs and tutorials for students; let slides prioritize high-level
						information only; use slide speaker notes or a separate lecture document entirely (only for the
						instructor's eyes) to ensure students only receive exactly the information they need.
					</li>
					<li>
						Actually use the critical concepts checklist that we already have for each topic – these existed before,
						but were not consulted at all when creating slides for lectures. We had the perfect tool to keep our
						slides focused, but had forgotten to use it!
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					Other improvements included splitting multi-topic slide decks into separate decks, and recording more
					walkthrough videos as learning material for both preparing lecturers, and studying students. I also
					implemented a templated slide design, tailored for each program, to ensure visual consistency happened by
					default, without any additional effort needed by slide creators.
				</p>
				<p>The effects of these changes were palpable both to the Instruction teams, and the Product team.</p>
				<p>
					For the Product team: we no longer wasted time adding information to slides needlessly, without the above
					qualifiers in mind. If we had not done this research, this had been the original planned work to be
					undertaken by our curriculum designers.
				</p>
				<p>
					For the instruction team: higher quality material was provided for them to prepare from, and slide
					information overload was avoided, empowering instructors to focus their energy on more bespoke, effective
					support for students during workshops and lectures. They were no longer bogged down or reduced to reciting
					steps from a dense slide deck.
				</p>
				<p>
					I was able to observe these positive changes concretely, as well as through feedback from my manager and
					Product team lead. These improvements were made not only to the B2C bootcamp materials and processes, but
					also for our enterprise B2B clients - wide-scale adoption meant wide-scale impact.
				</p>
				<p><strong>What would I improve, for next time?</strong></p>
				<p>
					As is often the case in a fast-paced, start-up environment where you are the sole designer, I quickly had to
					pivot away from this project to another one before I had a chance to implement more rigorous measures of
					success. If I had a chance to revisit this project, I would have liked to share a follow-up feedback survey
					with students, instructors, and Product team members to collect more data that could be used to
					quantitatively measure the success of this project. And if there was a chance to work with even just one
					other designer in this process, I would have divided our responsibilities and had us both review research
					materials in order to minimize the chance of bias creeping in.
				</p>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<div class="cta-wrap">
				<a class="cta-button" href="/">&larr; Home</a>
			</div>
		</main>
	</div>
	<SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.hero {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		padding: var(--space-padding-large);
		gap: var(--space-padding-medium);
	}

	.back-link {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: var(--space-micro);
		width: fit-content;
		text-decoration: none;
		color: var(--color-dark-gray);
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: var(--text-micro);
		transition: color 0.2s ease;
	}

	.back-link:hover {
		color: var(--color-medium-green);
	}

	.hero__body {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-padding-large);
	}

	.hero__heading {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-medium);
	}

	.hero__heading h1 {
		margin: 0;
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: var(--text-h1);
		font-weight: 500;
	}

	.hero__company {
		font-size: var(--text-micro);
		color: var(--color-light-grey);
	}

	.hero__image {
		aspect-ratio: 2000 / 1125;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-dark-gray);
		border-radius: 5px;
		overflow: hidden;
	}

	.hero__image img {
		width: 100%;
		height: 100%;
		object-fit: cover;
	}

	.hero__body hr {
		width: 100%;
		height: 1px;
		margin: 0;
		border: none;
		background-color: var(--color-light-grey);
		border-radius: 5px;
	}

	.hero__body p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: var(--text-body);
		line-height: 1.5;
	}

	.hero__skills {
		font-size: var(--text-micro);
		color: var(--color-light-grey);
	}

	.summary {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-padding-medium);
		padding: var(--space-padding-large);
	}

	.summary h2 {
		margin: 0;
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: var(--text-h2);
		font-weight: 500;
	}

	.summary p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: var(--text-body);
		line-height: 1.5;
	}

	.summary ul {
		margin: 0;
		padding-left: 1.2em;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-medium);
		font-size: var(--text-body);
		line-height: 1.5;
	}

	.cta-wrap {
		padding: 0 var(--space-padding-large) var(--space-padding-large);
	}

	.cta-button {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		height: 33px;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-dark-gray);
		border-radius: 18px;
		background-color: var(--color-light-purple);
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: var(--text-micro);
		text-decoration: none;
		color: var(--color-dark-gray);
		transition: filter 0.2s ease;
	}

	.cta-button:hover {
		filter: brightness(0.93);
	}
</style>
